  "body": {
    "id": "lem:additive-reduction",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "If \\(\\mu_{cx}=a_c+b_x\\) on \\(E_+\\), every selector has \\(\\theta_h=\\sum_cp_ca_c+\\sum_xr_xb_x\\). Under full support this reduces to the standard version-irrelevance case in which changing a calibrated conditional version law cannot change the macro mean.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mu",
      "E_+",
      "\\mathcal C",
      "\\mathcal X",
      "s",
      "\\theta_h",
      "h",
      "p",
      "r"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:effective-support",
      "lem:point-id-iff",
      "prop:sharp-envelope"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction checks the criterion against a familiar special case.",
    "gap": "The additive identity is classical; its use here is a sanity reduction for the selector envelope.",
    "consumer": "It gives a transparent empirical-table validation check.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume that on every effective edge there are potentials satisfying \\(\\mu_{cx}=a_c+b_x\\).  For any selector in \\(\\mathfrak A_{\\mathrm{free}}\\), Proposition~\\texttt{prop:sharp-envelope} associates the coupling \\(\\gamma_{cx}=p_cs_x(c)\\in\\Gamma(p,r)\\) and gives \\(\\theta_h=\\sum_{c,x}\\gamma_{cx}\\mu_{cx}\\).  A positive entry of \\(\\gamma\\) is, by Definition~\\texttt{def:effective-support}, an edge of \\(E_+\\).  Terms off \\(E_+\\) therefore have zero weight, and the margin equations yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta_h\n&=\\sum_{(c,x)\\in E_+}\\gamma_{cx}(a_c+b_x)\\\\\n&=\\sum_ca_c\\sum_x\\gamma_{cx}+\\sum_xb_x\\sum_c\\gamma_{cx}\\\\\n&=\\sum_cp_ca_c+\\sum_xr_xb_x.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe right-hand side contains no selector kernel.  This also follows from the potential characterization in Lemma~\\texttt{lem:point-id-iff}, which ensures \\(L=U\\).\n\nUnder full effective support, the additive representation holds on all of \\(\\mathcal C\\times\\mathcal X\\).  Any two calibrated conditional version laws \\(s\\) and \\(s'\\) have the same margins \\(p,r\\), so applying the displayed calculation to each gives\n\\[\n\\theta_h(s)=\\sum_cp_ca_c+\\sum_xr_xb_x=\\theta_h(s').\n\\]\nThus changing the calibrated conditional version law cannot change the macro mean, which is exactly the asserted version-irrelevance reduction.",
